<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Juan Carlos I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">b. 1938</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Juan Carlos de Borbón</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Juan Carlos I was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> from 1975 to 2014. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-franco">Francisco Franco</a> named him as his successor in 1969, but after Franco's death he backed the transition to democracy with Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolfo-suarez">Adolfo Suárez</a>, leading to the constitution of 1978. His televised stand against the attempted coup of February 1981 secured the new democracy. He abdicated in favour of his son Felipe VI.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/juan-carlos-i.jpg" alt="Juan Carlos I" width="320" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Irekia · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rey_Juan_Carlos_2013.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
